{"instruction_sha256":"30e1957fc0d939bf2eff852dc48a5c21826b4fecaead2eaffc45e4afcc161c7a","judge_config":{"claude":{"effort":"xhigh","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5"},"codex":{"effort":"xhigh","model":"gpt-6.1-sol"}},"kind":"grade","payload":{"passages":[{"passage_id":"187cb5482b5276f72e106bf40f90af6072921ec5b92d397119644d3c09995ca4","section_path":"Table of Contents","source_id":"riscv-privileged","text":"# **Table of Contents**\n\n|Preamble ................................................................................................................................................. 1|\n|---|\n|Preface.................................................................................................................................................... 2|\n|1. Introduction........................................................................................................................................ 9|\n|1.1. RISC-V Privileged Software Stack Terminology.............................................................................. 9|\n|1.2. Privilege Levels ............................................................................................................................ 10|\n|1.3. Debug Mode.................................................................................................................................. 11|\n|2. Control and Status Registers (CSRs)............................................................................................. 12|\n|2.1. CSR Address Mapping Conventions ............................................................................................. 12|\n|2.2. CSR Listing .................................................................................................................................. 13|\n|2.3. CSR Field Specifications............................................................................................................. 20|\n|2.3.1. Reserved Writes Preserve Values, Reads Ignore Values (WPRI) ........................................... 20|\n|2.3.2. Write/Read Only Legal Values (WLRL)................................................................................... 21|\n|2.3.3. Write Any Values, Reads Legal Values (WARL) ...................................................................... 21|\n|2.4. CSR Field Modulation .................................................................................................................. 21|\n|2.5. Implicit Reads of CSRs................................................................................................................ 22|\n|2.6. CSR Width Modulation................................................................................................................. 22|\n|2.7. Explicit Accesses to CSRs Wider than XLEN................................................................................ 22|\n|3. Machine-Level ISA, Version 1.13 ................................................................................................... 24|\n|3.1. Machine-Level CSRs .................................................................................................................... 24|\n|3.1.1. Machine ISA (misa) Register .................................................................................................. 24|\n|3.1.2. Machine Vendor ID (mvendorid) Register ................................................................................ 27|\n|3.1.3. Machine Architecture ID (marchid) Register........................................................................... 27|\n|3.1.4. Machine Implementation ID (mimpid) Register...................................................................... 28|\n|3.1.5. Hart ID (mhartid) Register...................................................................................................... 28|\n|3.1.6. Machine Status (mstatus and mstatush) Registers................................................................... 29|\n|3.1.6.1. Privilege and Global Interrupt-Enable Stack in mstatus register...................................... 29|\n|3.1.6.2. Double Trap Control in mstatus Register.......................................................................... 31|\n|3.1.6.3. Base ISA Control in mstatus Register.............................................................................. 32|\n|3.1.6.4. Memory Privilege in mstatus Register ............................................................................. 32|\n|3.1.6.5. Endianness Control in mstatus and mstatush Registers.................................................... 33|\n|3.1.6.6. Virtualization Support in mstatus Register ...................................................................... 34|\n|3.1.6.7. Extension Context Status in mstatus Register ................................................................. 35|\n|3.1.6.8. Previous Expected Landing Pad (ELP) State in mstatus Register.................................... 39|\n|3.1.7. Machine Trap-Vector Base-Address (mtvec) Register............................................................. 39|\n|3.1.8. Machine Trap Delegation (medeleg and mideleg) Registers .................................................... 40|"},{"passage_id":"2cdbf44ccb0648a984ec84ab35b38f3f5fda7062a4d012ab75718f4e1178be93","section_path":"Chapter 2. Control and Status Registers (CSRs) > 2.3. CSR Field Specifications > 2.3.1. Reserved Writes Preserve Values, Reads Ignore Values (WPRI)","source_id":"riscv-privileged","text":"### **2.3.1. Reserved Writes Preserve Values, Reads Ignore Values (WPRI)**\n\nSome whole read/write fields are reserved for future use. Software should ignore the values read from these fields, and should preserve the values held in these fields when writing values to other fields of the same register. For forward compatibility, implementations that do not furnish these fields must make them read-only zero. These fields are labeled **WPRI** in the register descriptions.\n\n_To simplify the software model, any backward-compatible future definition of previously reserved fields within a CSR must cope with the possibility that a non-_  _atomic read/modify/write sequence is used to update other fields in the CSR.Alternatively, the original CSR definition must specify that subfields can only be updated atomically, which may require a two-instruction clear bit/set bit sequence in general that can be problematic if intermediate values are not legal._"},{"passage_id":"37a25fefbaf05030a0d0f1b4fe18c12b5f3ac51e2d20904df9f8c9a73d3f5175","section_path":"Chapter 19. \"CMO\" Extensions for Base Cache Management Operation ISA, Version 1.0.0 > 19.5. Control and Status Register State > // illegal-instruction exceptions","source_id":"riscv-unprivileged","text":"Each `xenvcfg` register is WARL; however, software should determine the legal values from the execution environment discovery mechanism."},{"passage_id":"3e0ec4be1886a42ae1d6e502c8079a43caf94f922ef54f57ac69a0ad763b66ff","section_path":"Chapter 2. Control and Status Registers (CSRs) > 2.3. CSR Field Specifications","source_id":"riscv-privileged","text":"## **2.3. CSR Field Specifications**\n\nThe following definitions and abbreviations are used in specifying the behavior of fields within the CSRs."}],"query":{"id":"q053","intent":"Distinguish the software obligations and read/write guarantees of WPRI, WLRL, and WARL CSR fields.","text":"What do WPRI, WLRL and WARL mean for RISC-V CSR fields?"}},"pilot":false,"schema":{"$defs":{"Grade":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"architecture_tags":{"items":{"enum":["riscv","loongarch","x86","other"],"type":"string"},"title":"Architecture Tags","type":"array"},"grade":{"maximum":3,"minimum":0,"title":"Grade","type":"integer"},"passage_id":{"minLength":1,"title":"Passage Id","type":"string"},"quote":{"title":"Quote","type":"string"},"reason":{"minLength":1,"title":"Reason","type":"string"}},"required":["passage_id","grade","reason","quote","architecture_tags"],"title":"Grade","type":"object"}},"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"grades":{"items":{"$ref":"#/$defs/Grade"},"title":"Grades","type":"array"}},"required":["grades"],"title":"GradeAnswer","type":"object"},"schema_version":3,"sha256":"080b4151e00a1fdd706aac8ca6d2f358ff6ef73058a71731ecad4f98058e27e5","system_sha256":"0731bfffd8dfd9b1a7f3394b7f28e0c875dbee813b796de4afe0936d756fea63"}
